<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siete Leyes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1836</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Seven Laws</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siete Leyes were seven constitutional laws adopted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1835–1836 that replaced the federal Constitution of 1824 with a centralist system. The states became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> under governors appointed from the capital. Opposition to them helped provoke the Texas Revolution and other regional revolts, and they were set aside in the 1840s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siete-leyes.jpg" alt="Siete Leyes" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rainier Blue · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siete_Leyes_Illustrated.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
